course: religion-ethics
lesson: 61
title: When a friend's mum or dad dies: what to say and do
level: primary-4
stars: 3
explain: When a friend's mum or dad dies, there are no perfect words, and most people are afraid of saying the wrong thing; but a small, honest «I'm really sorry about your mum» is far better than saying nothing and drifting away.
explain: Picture Grace on the day Lily comes back to school after her mum died: Grace does not make a speech, she says «I'm sorry, I'm here if you want to talk», sits next to Lily at lunch, and then just carries on being a normal friend.
explain: Listen more and talk less: «at least you still have your dad» can make a friend feel their loss doesn't count, and nosy questions like «did you see the body?» are better replaced with «do you want to talk about it?», letting the grieving friend lead.
explain: A grieving friend may laugh and joke one day and cry in class the next, especially on days like Mother's Day; both are normal, so we do not judge, and we stand up for them if others do.
explain: Every family has its own customs: a Hindu family may pray at home for thirteen days, an Irish family may hold a wake where neighbours say «sorry for your trouble», a Jewish family may sit shiva; in the Bible, Job's friends did best when they simply sat with him in silence.
fact: There are no perfect words; «I'm so sorry about your mum» and «I'm here if you want to talk» are good, simple things to say.
fact: Sentences starting «at least» can make a grieving person feel their loss doesn't count, even when they are meant kindly.
fact: A small gift like a card, a drawing or a favourite snack shows you care; big presents are not needed.
fact: Grieving children often swing between sadness and being normal; laughing and playing is a rest from sadness, not a sign they don't care.
fact: Special days like Mother's Day and Father's Day can be very hard for a child whose parent has died.
fact: It is kinder to let a grieving person choose what to share than to ask for details.
fact: Many Hindu families hold prayers and gatherings for about thirteen days after a death; Hindus believe the soul moves on to a new life.
fact: Sports teams often wear black armbands or hold a minute's silence or applause to show respect and support.
fact: Avoiding a grieving friend can make them feel lonely; «I didn't know what to say, but I'm here» is enough.
fact: In Ireland a wake is often held at home, where people visit, pray, chat and share food; mourners say «sorry for your trouble», and nobody has to look at the body.
fact: The Bible tells Christians to «weep with those who weep»; Job's friends sat with him in silence for seven days before speaking.
fact: You do not have to fix a friend's sadness; listening and saying «that sounds really hard» helps.
exercise: Lily comes back to school after her mum died. Grace asks, «What do I even say?» Best answer? | «I'm so sorry about your mum», then be a normal friend | Nothing at all, because talking about it only makes it much worse | A long speech about how death is just a natural part of life | «Cheer up, it happens to everyone in the end, you know»
exercise: Ben tells Ollie, «At least you still have your mum.» Why wasn't that the best thing to say? | «At least» can make Ollie feel his loss doesn't count | Because Ollie doesn't have a mum either | Because you should never mention parents at school | It was perfect and Ollie should be grateful
exercise: Zara wants to give Lily something. What is a good idea? | A card, a drawing or her favourite snack | An expensive present costing lots of money | Nothing, because gifts are rude after a death | A photo of Lily's mum without asking
exercise: Jayden jokes a week after his dad died; a kid says, «He doesn't even care.» What is true? | Playing and laughing is a rest from sadness, not a sign he doesn't care | The kid is right, Jayden didn't love his dad | Jayden must be forced to be sad at school | Jayden is just pretending that nothing happened
exercise: Lily cries during the Mother's Day card lesson. What can Grace do? | Sit with her, pass a tissue, and tell the teacher if she needs help | Pretend not to notice anything and just keep making her own card | Tell her quietly to stop crying, because they are in class | Make silly jokes so that the whole class laughs and forgets all about it
exercise: Callum asks Sam, «Did you see the body? Was it scary?» What would be gentler? | «Do you want to talk about it?», letting Sam choose | «Tell me every single detail about the body, I won't tell» | Asking the very same thing again, only a bit louder | Asking other people what happened instead of Sam himself
exercise: Priya's Hindu family will pray at home for thirteen days. What should Hannah do? | Ask Priya's family or her own parents what is welcome | Turn up at the house every day without being invited | Stay away completely, because she is not a Hindu herself | Tell Priya kindly that the Christian ways are better ones
exercise: What do Hindus believe happens to the soul after death? | It moves on to a new life | It sleeps in the grave until spring | It becomes an animal at once, always | Nothing happens to it at all
exercise: A month later Lily says she misses her mum most at bedtime. What can Grace say? | «That sounds really hard», and just listen | «You really should be over it by now, it's been a month» | «Just try not to think about her when you go to bed» | «My hamster died last year, so I know exactly how you feel»
exercise: The coach tells the team Leo's mum died. How can the team help? | Wear black armbands or hold a minute's silence | Pretend it didn't happen and play on | Ask Leo to explain at half-time | Cancel the team for the rest of the season
exercise: Jade has been avoiding her friend since the friend's brother died. What should she do? | Go back and say, «I didn't know what to say, but I'm here» | Keep avoiding her until her friend cheers up by herself | Find a new best friend instead and let the old one be | Send her a funny joke to make her forget all about her brother
exercise: Aidan from Dublin is going to a wake. Does he have to look at the body? | No, he can stay close to his mam and just say «sorry for your trouble» | Yes, everyone who goes to a wake must touch the body, even children | Yes, or the whole family will be offended and ask him to leave | No, because children are never allowed in to an Irish wake at all, by custom
exercise: What do people say to the family at an Irish wake? | «Sorry for your trouble» | «Happy days to you all» | «Better luck next time» | «Rest easy, we'll be off»
exercise: What did Job's friends in the Bible do that helped most? | They sat with him in silence | They told him it was his own fault | They gave him lots of advice at once | They left him alone for a year
exercise: What does the Bible tell Christians to do with people who weep? | Weep with them | Tell them to stop | Leave them to it | Pray they weep less
exercise: Why is saying something small better than avoiding a grieving friend? | Being ignored can make them feel lonely | Talking makes grief disappear at once | Small words fix all the sadness | Friends never notice being avoided
test: Ollie's mate, a Gen Z teen, says, «Bro, at least you've still got your mum, so it's not that deep.» («It's not that deep» means «it's no big deal».) What is the better thing to say? | «I'm really sorry about your dad», then listen | Say «at least» again, only louder this time so he hears | «Your dad was kinda mid anyway, so it's fine, bro» | Nothing at all, and quietly stop being his friend
test: What would happen if everyone in Lily's class avoided her because they didn't know what to say? | She would feel lonely on top of her grief | She would feel much better left alone by all | She would forget about her mum faster | Nothing at all would change for her
test: A kid in Jayden's class sneers, «He's lowkey fine, he's laughing, he never cared.» («Lowkey» means «quietly, a bit».) How should Mia answer? | Grief comes and goes; laughing is a rest, so leave him be | She agrees, because real grief means never laughing at all | She goes and tells Jayden to stop laughing so much in class | She laughs at Jayden too, so that he sees how it feels
test: An Aussie mate asks, «Should I rock up at Priya's with heaps of lollies, or what?» («Rock up» means «turn up»; «lollies» means «sweets».) What is the wise move? | Ask Priya's family or your parents what is welcome first | Turn up at the house with heaps of lollies every single day | Stay away for ever, because it's not your faith anyway | Send a big, expensive present to the house instead of going
test: A Belfast lad says, «Your man's da died, I'm not going near him, I'd only say something eejit.» («Eejit» means «idiot, stupid».) What is the wise advice? | Go anyway; «I'm sorry, I don't know what to say» is enough | Stay away, so that you don't say anything stupid or wrong | Wait for a whole year until he is better, then talk to him | Send someone else in your place to talk to him for you
test: Aidan's mam tells him, «We'll go to the house to pay our respects, love.» Aidan whispers, «I don't wanna see a dead body.» What is true? | He can stay close to his mam and needn't look at the body | He has to touch the body or it's rude | Children can never go to a wake | The body will talk to the visitors
test: A Texan boy says, «Y'all, Leo's mama passed, can we wear black armbands Saturday?» («Passed» means «died».) What will this show? | That the team cares and Leo is not alone | That the team is sad they might lose | That black armbands are the new kit | That Leo must play even though he is sad
test: Lily's teacher, formally: «Those of you who wish may prepare a card for another person dear to you.» Why does she say this? | So Lily and others can still take part on a hard day | Because Mother's Day has been cancelled across the country by law | Because Lily is not allowed to make any card at all this year | Because only grown-ups may get cards, not children at school
test: Callum's brother says, «Asking about the body is fine, everyone wants to know, innit.» What is the most thoughtful reply? | Curiosity is natural, but let the grieving friend choose what to share | He's right; always ask for all the details, everyone wants them | Never speak to a grieving friend at all, about anything | Ask the teacher for photographs of the body instead of asking the friend
test: An ethics olympiad judge asks: «Ben meant to help with "at least". Does a good intention make the words good?» What is the best-reasoned answer? | Ben's intention was good, but words can still hurt; next time «I'm sorry» and listening is better | Yes, a good intention makes any words perfect | No, Ben is a bad person for saying it | It doesn't matter, because words never hurt anyone
test: Jade says, «I just kinda stopped talking to her, is that OK?» What is honest and kind? | It's understandable, but go back; «I'm here» is enough | Yes, it's always best to leave grieving people alone for good | No, and now she can never be friends with her again | Yes, because her friend has lots of other friends to help
test: A cartoon shows a friend fixing a grieving girl's sadness in one speech, and she skips off happy. What is the real picture? | Grief takes time; friends can listen but cannot fix it in one go | One good speech ends grief for ever | Grieving people are never sad for long | Only cartoons have grieving people
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises an appropriate initial communication to a bereaved schoolfellow? | A brief and sincere expression of sorrow, followed by attentive listening | A lengthy discourse upon the inevitability of mortality for all persons | The avoidance of any communication whatsoever with the bereaved pupil hereafter | An enquiry as to the precise circumstances and details of the death
exam: Identify the principal deficiency of consolation commencing with the words «at least». | It may diminish the significance of the bereaved person's loss | It is grammatically incorrect | It is excessively brief | It is forbidden by religious law
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning mourning customs observed by many Hindu families. | Prayers and gatherings at home for approximately thirteen days | Burial of the deceased within a single hour | Abstention from any form of prayer | The immediate resumption of ordinary affairs without observance
exam: Ascertain the conduct described in the Book of Job as most consoling to the afflicted. | The silent presence of his friends beside him | The provision of lengthy explanations of his suffering | The withdrawal of his friends from his company | The attribution of blame to the sufferer
